{"content_hash": "d10a91c295c05fe1", "version_id": 514, "article_id": 52, "level_code": "en_b2", "lang": "en", "level": 4, "level_label": "B2 中高级", "title": "UK court clears ex-Deutsche Bank trader in rate-rigging case", "title_zh": "英国法院推翻德银前交易员利率操纵定罪", "has_translation": true, "topic": "business", "source": "bbc_business", "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqgkvj10k6lno?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1620266757065-5814239881fd?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8Mnx8ZGV1dHNjaGUlMjBiYW5rJTIwdHJhZGVyfGVufDB8MHx8fDE3OTE2Nzc5NDl8MA&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by Kanchanara on Unsplash", "author": "Kanchanara", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@kanchanara?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/person-using-black-laptop-computer-5hcV51EeeWc?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(271, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(309, 46%, 48%)", "label": "BUSINESS", "seed": "14bf80248d84"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-09", "word_count": 225, "reading_minutes": 1.1, "lead": "A former Deutsche Bank trader who was jailed in 2018 for rigging interest rates has had his conviction overturned. The decision comes after a long legal battle and follows similar rulings for other bankers.", "lead_zh": "一名因操纵利率于2018年入狱的德意志银行前交易员，其定罪已被推翻。这一裁决是在漫长的法律斗争之后作出的，此前已有其他银行家获得类似判决。", "preview": "Christian Bittar, a former Deutsche Bank trader, was jailed in 2018 for manipulating Euribor, a key interest rate. On 9 October 2026, the Co", "preview_zh": "克里斯蒂安·比塔尔是德意志银行前交易员，2018年因操纵欧元银行间同业拆借利率（Euribor）这一关键利率而入狱。2026年10月9日，上诉法院推翻了其定罪。比塔尔因未获签证，通过视频从瑞士观看庭审，他告诉BBC，他为这一天等了很久很久。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 502, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/52/en_b2.mp3", "duration": 90.562, "size_bytes": 547056, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 253, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@fc48a26/content/data/versions/514.json", "paragraphs": ["Christian Bittar, a former Deutsche Bank trader, was jailed in 2018 for manipulating Euribor, a key interest rate. On 9 October 2026, the Court of Appeal quashed his conviction. Bittar, who watched via video link from Switzerland because he was not granted a visa, told the BBC he had waited a very long time for this day.", "The ruling follows the same court's decision earlier this week to overturn five convictions of ex-Barclays bankers. In total, 19 City traders were convicted in the US and UK between 2015 and 2019. Now, 18 of them have been acquitted. Only one former trader remains convicted: Peter Johnson, a former Barclays trader and whistleblower, who has applied to appeal.", "The Libor scandal erupted in 2012, when it was discovered that banks had misrepresented their positions during the financial crisis to boost profits and hide difficulties. The news has led lawyers and politicians to demand that the Bank of England and the Treasury release all records about their own roles, amid concerns of a cover-up.", "Bittar said: 'Finally the injustice of what I and others suffered has been recognised.' His wife Caroline said their family had lost 15 valuable years. Former minister David Davis told the BBC the traders were victims of a scapegoating exercise arising from the government's own misbehaviour in lowballing Libor rates to rescue the economy."], "paragraphs_zh": ["克里斯蒂安·比塔尔是德意志银行前交易员，2018年因操纵欧元银行间同业拆借利率（Euribor）这一关键利率而入狱。2026年10月9日，上诉法院推翻了其定罪。比塔尔因未获签证，通过视频从瑞士观看庭审，他告诉BBC，他为这一天等了很久很久。", "这一裁决之前，同一法院本周早些时候推翻了五名巴克莱银行前银行家的定罪。2015年至2019年间，共有19名伦敦金融城交易员在美英两国被定罪。如今，其中18人已被宣判无罪。仅剩一名前交易员仍被定罪：巴克莱前交易员、举报人彼得·约翰逊，他已申请上诉。", "Libor丑闻于2012年爆发，当时人们发现银行在金融危机爆发时在设定拆借利率过程中虚报头寸，以抬高利润并掩盖困境。这一消息促使律师和政界人士要求英国央行和财政部公布所有关于自身角色的记录，因为有人担心存在掩盖行为。", "比塔尔表示：“我和其他人遭受的不公终于得到承认。”他的妻子卡罗琳说，他们家失去了15年宝贵时光。前部长戴维·戴维斯告诉BBC，这些交易员是政府自身不当行为的替罪羊，政府当年为了拯救经济而人为压低Libor利率。"], "body": "Christian Bittar, a former Deutsche Bank trader, was jailed in 2018 for manipulating Euribor, a key interest rate. On 9 October 2026, the Court of Appeal quashed his conviction. Bittar, who watched via video link from Switzerland because he was not granted a visa, told the BBC he had waited a very long time for this day.\n\nThe ruling follows the same court's decision earlier this week to overturn five convictions of ex-Barclays bankers. In total, 19 City traders were convicted in the US and UK between 2015 and 2019. Now, 18 of them have been acquitted. Only one former trader remains convicted: Peter Johnson, a former Barclays trader and whistleblower, who has applied to appeal.\n\nThe Libor scandal erupted in 2012, when it was discovered that banks had misrepresented their positions during the financial crisis to boost profits and hide difficulties. The news has led lawyers and politicians to demand that the Bank of England and the Treasury release all records about their own roles, amid concerns of a cover-up.\n\nBittar said: 'Finally the injustice of what I and others suffered has been recognised.' His wife Caroline said their family had lost 15 valuable years. Former minister David Davis told the BBC the traders were victims of a scapegoating exercise arising from the government's own misbehaviour in lowballing Libor rates to rescue the economy.", "body_zh": "克里斯蒂安·比塔尔是德意志银行前交易员，2018年因操纵欧元银行间同业拆借利率（Euribor）这一关键利率而入狱。2026年10月9日，上诉法院推翻了其定罪。比塔尔因未获签证，通过视频从瑞士观看庭审，他告诉BBC，他为这一天等了很久很久。\n\n这一裁决之前，同一法院本周早些时候推翻了五名巴克莱银行前银行家的定罪。2015年至2019年间，共有19名伦敦金融城交易员在美英两国被定罪。如今，其中18人已被宣判无罪。仅剩一名前交易员仍被定罪：巴克莱前交易员、举报人彼得·约翰逊，他已申请上诉。\n\nLibor丑闻于2012年爆发，当时人们发现银行在金融危机爆发时在设定拆借利率过程中虚报头寸，以抬高利润并掩盖困境。这一消息促使律师和政界人士要求英国央行和财政部公布所有关于自身角色的记录，因为有人担心存在掩盖行为。\n\n比塔尔表示：“我和其他人遭受的不公终于得到承认。”他的妻子卡罗琳说，他们家失去了15年宝贵时光。前部长戴维·戴维斯告诉BBC，这些交易员是政府自身不当行为的替罪羊，政府当年为了拯救经济而人为压低Libor利率。", "text": "Christian Bittar, a former Deutsche Bank trader, was jailed in 2018 for manipulating Euribor, a key interest rate. On 9 October 2026, the Court of Appeal quashed his conviction. Bittar, who watched via video link from Switzerland because he was not granted a visa, told the BBC he had waited a very long time for this day. The ruling follows the same court's decision earlier this week to overturn five convictions of ex-Barclays bankers. In total, 19 City traders were convicted in the US and UK between 2015 and 2019. Now, 18 of them have been acquitted. Only one former trader remains convicted: Peter Johnson, a former Barclays trader and whistleblower, who has applied to appeal. The Libor scandal erupted in 2012, when it was discovered that banks had misrepresented their positions during the financial crisis to boost profits and hide difficulties. The news has led lawyers and politicians to demand that the Bank of England and the Treasury release all records about their own roles, amid concerns of a cover-up. Bittar said: 'Finally the injustice of what I and others suffered has been recognised.' His wife Caroline said their family had lost 15 valuable years. Former minister David Davis told the BBC the traders were victims of a scapegoating exercise arising from the government's own misbehaviour in lowballing Libor rates to rescue the economy.", "vocab": [{"word": "conviction", "pos": "noun", "zh": "定罪", "note": "", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "overturn", "pos": "verb", "zh": "推翻", "note": "常用于法律语境，如推翻判决", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "quash", "pos": "verb", "zh": "撤销，废除", "note": "法律用语，指撤销定罪或判决", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "benchmark", "pos": "noun", "zh": "基准", "note": "", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "manipulate", "pos": "verb", "zh": "操纵", "note": "", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "acquit", "pos": "verb", "zh": "宣判无罪", "note": "", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "whistleblower", "pos": "noun", "zh": "举报人", "note": "", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "backlash", "pos": "noun", "zh": "强烈反对", "note": "", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "bailout", "pos": "noun", "zh": "纾困，救助", "note": "", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "scapegoat", "pos": "verb", "zh": "使成为替罪羊", "note": "名词形式为 scapegoat", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "miscarriage of justice", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "误判，司法不公", "note": "", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "suppress", "pos": "verb", "zh": "压制，隐瞒", "note": "", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "Ex-Deutsche Bank trader jailed for rigging rates has conviction overturned", "summary_original": "The Court of Appeal quashes the conviction of Christian Bittar, a former Deutsche Bank trader jailed in 2018.", "body_original": "Ex-Deutsche Bank trader jailed for rigging rates has conviction overturned\nBy\nAndy Verity\nFinancial investigations correspondent\nPublished\n9 October 2026\nAnother former banker sentenced in one of the biggest scandals of the financial crisis has had his conviction overturned following a long-running legal battle.\nThe Court of Appeal has quashed the conviction of Christian Bittar, a former Deutsche Bank trader who went to prison in 2018 for \"manipulating\" the benchmark interest rate, Euribor.\nIt comes after the same UK court overturned five convictions of ex-Barclays bankers in the rate rigging trials earlier this week.\nBittar, who watched proceedings via video link from Switzerland because he was not granted a visa to attend court, told the BBC: \"I have waited a very, very long time for this day.\"\nThe financial crisis began in 2008, sending huge economic shockwaves across the world and triggering recessions in many countries.\nThere was a public backlash against bankers, held by many to be responsible for the crisis, while the financial sector was protected by taxpayer-funded bailouts.\nThe Libor scandal erupted in 2012, when it was discovered that at the outbreak of the financial crisis, banks had been misrepresenting their positions during the process of setting the lending rate, helping to boost profits and mask difficulties.\nSome 19 City traders were convicted in the US and UK between 2015 and 2019 across nine criminal trials held in London and New York.\nEighteen of these have now been acquitted.\nOnly one former trader in the world now remains convicted: former Barclays trader Peter Johnson, who was also one of the original whistleblowers in the interest rate rigging scandal. He pleaded guilty on advice that he had little chance at winning at trial. The court confirmed that he too has now applied to appeal against his conviction.\nThe news has already prompted lawyers and senior politicians to press for the Bank of England and the Treasury to release all their records about their own roles in interest rate 'rigging' during the financial crisis, amid concerns raised of a cover-up of the role of central banks and governments.\n'Finally our injustice has been recognised'\nSpeaking after his conviction was quashed, Bittar said: \"Finally the injustice of what I and others suffered has been recognised.\n\"I am so grateful for those who stood by me through this ordeal and those who worked so tirelessly to correct it.\"\nHis wife, Caroline, who lives in the UK, said their family had lost 15 \"valuable years\", with their children \"growing up with this injustice\".\nShe added they were looking forward to \"enjoying life with our family and friends without this shadow hanging over us\".\nOn Wednesday, Jay Merchant, Jonathan Mathew, Alex Pabon, Colin Bermingham and Philippe Moryoussef saw their convictions for 'manipulating' the interest rate benchmarks Libor and Euribor quashed after a 10-year struggle for justice.\nThey launched fresh appeals against their jail sentences following a Supreme Court ruling last year that acquitted Tom Hayes, the first to be jailed for 'rigging' interest rates in 2015, and Carlo Palombo, jailed in 2019.\nTom Hayes, who was initially sentenced to 14 years, reduced on appeal to 11, is now seeking damages from his former employer UBS.\nThe traders were among 37 City traders and brokers prosecuted for \"manipulating\" the interest rate benchmarks Libor and Euribor, which track the cost of borrowing cash between the banks and have been used to set the interest rates on millions of mortgages and commercial loans.\nThe BBC has uncovered evidence of a much larger, state-led 'rigging' of interest rates, under pressure from central banks and governments across the world. Evidence implicating Downing Street and the Bank of England was suppressed throughout the criminal trials.\nFormer Conservative cabinet minister David Davis told the BBC the innocent traders had been the victims of a series of miscarriages of justice.\nHe said the people exonerated this week and before were \"the victims of a scapegoating exercise which arose as a result of the government's own misbehaviour in lowballing [falsely understating] Libor interest rates themselves, in order to try and rescue the economy after their own self-induced crash in the late 2000s\".\nIn 2015, during the first trial for interest rate 'rigging', of former UBS trader Hayes, Mr Justice Jeremy Cooke decided as a matter of law that any attempt to influence the rates could not be lawful, and that any rate influenced by commercial interest must automatically be false. Judges in the subsequent eight trials followed suit.\nHowever, in July 2025, the Supreme Court decided that all the judges had erred, misdirecting juries that it was wrong as a matter of law. Instead, whether the requests were right or wrong was properly a matter of fact for the jury to decide, not the judge.\nAll convictions in the United States were overturned in 2022 after an appeal court ruled that there was insufficient evidence that the traders' requests had broken any rules or laws.\nRelated topics\nUK Supreme Court\nDeutsche Bank\nBarclays\nSerious Fraud Office\nBanking\nMore on this story\nEx-bankers jailed for rigging rates have convictions quashed\nPublished\n3 days ago\nRate 'rigging' traders say they were scapegoated - now the Supreme Court will decide\nPublished\n26 May 2025", "fetched_at": "2026-10-11T00:19:08.412879", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "Christian", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.562, "sm": 100, "em": 562, "cs": 0, "ce": 9, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "Bittar", "s": 0.575, "e": 1.075, "sm": 575, "em": 1075, "cs": 10, "ce": 16, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": ",", "s": 1.075, "e": 1.3, "sm": 1075, "em": 1300, "cs": 16, "ce": 17, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "a", "s": 1.3, "e": 1.35, "sm": 1300, "em": 1350, "cs": 18, "ce": 19, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "former", "s": 1.363, "e": 1.712, "sm": 1362, "em": 1712, "cs": 20, "ce": 26, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "Deutsche", "s": 1.725, "e": 2.087, "sm": 1725, "em": 2087, "cs": 27, "ce": 35, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "Bank", "s": 2.1, "e": 2.325, "sm": 2100, "em": 2325, "cs": 36, "ce": 40, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "trader", "s": 2.337, "e": 2.712, "sm": 2337, "em": 2712, "cs": 41, "ce": 47, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": ",", "s": 2.712, "e": 3.0, "sm": 2712, "em": 3000, "cs": 47, "ce": 48, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "was", "s": 3.0, "e": 3.188, "sm": 3000, "em": 3187, "cs": 49, "ce": 52, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "jailed", "s": 3.2, "e": 3.588, "sm": 3200, "em": 3587, "cs": 53, "ce": 59, "si": 0}, {"i": 11, "w": "in", "s": 3.6, "e": 3.975, "sm": 3600, "em": 3975, "cs": 60, "ce": 62, "si": 0}, {"i": 12, "w": "2018", "s": 3.975, "e": 4.725, "sm": 3975, "em": 4725, "cs": 63, "ce": 67, "si": 0}, {"i": 13, "w": "for", "s": 4.737, "e": 4.875, "sm": 4737, "em": 4875, "cs": 68, "ce": 71, "si": 0}, {"i": 14, "w": "manipulating", "s": 4.888, "e": 5.55, "sm": 4887, "em": 5550, "cs": 72, "ce": 84, "si": 0}, {"i": 15, "w": "Euribor", "s": 5.562, "e": 6.112, "sm": 5562, "em": 6112, "cs": 85, "ce": 92, "si": 0}, {"i": 16, "w": ",", "s": 6.112, "e": 6.312, "sm": 6112, "em": 6312, "cs": 92, "ce": 93, "si": 0}, {"i": 17, "w": "a", "s": 6.312, "e": 6.375, "sm": 6312, "em": 6375, "cs": 94, "ce": 95, "si": 0}, {"i": 18, "w"